          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 06 Follow-Up Sequences -->
    <section class="guide-section" id="followup">
      <div class="max-w-[800px] mx-auto px-6">
        <div class="font-mono text-[11px] font-medium uppercase tracking-[.1em] text-accent mb-2">06 &mdash; Follow-Ups</div>
        <h2 class="font-display font-bold text-[clamp(24px,3.5vw,32px)] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink mb-2">Converting silence into conversations</h2>
        <div class="tldr">
          <ul class="list-none p-0 m-0 space-y-1">
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Most positive responses come from follow-up 1 or 2, not the initial message</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Three touches, spaced 3&ndash;7 days apart, is the right rhythm</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">The graceful close leaves the door open without burning goodwill</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">The majority of creators send a single outreach message, receive no reply, and conclude the brand is not interested. This is almost always wrong. Marketing teams are overwhelmed, inboxes are crowded, and your email likely arrived during a sprint, a product launch, or simply a busy Tuesday. Following up is not pushy &mdash; it is professional. What crosses the line is following up five times in four days.</p>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">A clean three-touch follow-up sequence, properly spaced, will recover a significant share of opportunities that would otherwise evaporate.</p>

        <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-border bg-card mb-6">
          <table class="w-full border-collapse text-[14px]">
            <thead class="bg-bg">
              <tr>
                <th class="font-display font-semibold text-[13px] px-4 py-3 text-left border-b border-border">Touch</th>
                <th class="font-display font-semibold text-[13px] px-4 py-3 text-left border-b border-border">Timing</th>
                <th class="font-display font-semibold text-[13px] px-4 py-3 text-left border-b border-border">Purpose</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink">Follow-Up 1</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">3&ndash;4 days after pitch</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">Gentle resurface &mdash; re-share portfolio</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink">Follow-Up 2</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">~1 week later</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">Reduce barrier &mdash; offer to send rates directly</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-ink">Follow-Up 3</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-ink-2">Final message</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-ink-2">Graceful close &mdash; leave door open</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">Follow-Up 1: The Gentle Resurface</h3>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-2">Send 3&ndash;4 days after the initial pitch.</p>
        <blockquote class="border-l-[3px] border-accent pl-5 py-3 my-6 bg-accent-bg rounded-r-lg">
          <p class="text-ink-2 mb-0 text-[15px] leading-[1.7]">
            Hi [Name],<br><br>
            Wanted to make sure my earlier message did not get lost &mdash; I still think there is a strong alignment between [Brand] and the type of content I create. Portfolio here if it helps: [link].<br><br>
            Happy to share rates whenever convenient.<br><br>
            Best,<br>
            [Your Name]
          </p>
        </blockquote>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">Follow-Up 2: Reduce the Barrier</h3>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-2">Send about one week after Follow-Up 1.</p>
        <blockquote class="border-l-[3px] border-accent pl-5 py-3 my-6 bg-accent-bg rounded-r-lg">
          <p class="text-ink-2 mb-0 text-[15px] leading-[1.7]">
            Hi [Name],<br><br>
            One more quick note. If creator content is on your radar right now, I am happy to send rates directly &mdash; no call needed unless that is easier for you.<br><br>
            And if the timing is not right, completely understood. No hard feelings.<br><br>
            Best,<br>
            [Your Name]
          </p>
        </blockquote>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">Follow-Up 3: The Graceful Close</h3>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-2">Final message in the sequence.</p>
        <blockquote class="border-l-[3px] border-accent pl-5 py-3 my-6 bg-accent-bg rounded-r-lg">
          <p class="text-ink-2 mb-0 text-[15px] leading-[1.7]">
            Hi [Name],<br><br>
            Wrapping up on this thread so I am not cluttering your inbox. If a UGC collaboration ever makes sense for [Brand] down the road, I would genuinely love to reconnect.<br><br>
            All the best,<br>
            [Your Name]
          </p>
        </blockquote>

        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">Three touches, each separated by several days, is the right rhythm. After the third, move on. The brands that respond will typically do so after touch one or two. The graceful close leaves the door open without burning any goodwill.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 07 Rate Negotiation -->
    <section class="guide-section" id="negotiation">
      <div class="max-w-[800px] mx-auto px-6">
        <div class="font-mono text-[11px] font-medium uppercase tracking-[.1em] text-accent mb-2">07 &mdash; Negotiation</div>
        <h2 class="font-display font-bold text-[clamp(24px,3.5vw,32px)] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink mb-2">How to talk money without underselling</h2>
        <div class="tldr">
          <ul class="list-none p-0 m-0 space-y-1">
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Name your price first &mdash; never respond with "whatever works for you"</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Price the content, not your audience size</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Present packages, not unit prices</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">More creators lose income by quoting too low than by getting turned down. Setting and communicating your rates with confidence is a skill that compounds over your entire career. Here are the principles we consistently see successful creators follow:</p>

        <ol class="list-none p-0 counter-reset-step space-y-0">
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Never respond with "whatever works for you."</strong> This signals uncertainty about your own value and invites the brand to anchor at the lowest number they can justify. Name your price first.</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Price the content, not your audience size.</strong> Brands paying for UGC are buying production quality, strong hooks, and content that performs in paid media. Your follower count is irrelevant to that equation.</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Charge a premium for usage rights.</strong> A video the brand will run as a paid ad for three to six months delivers far more value than a single organic post. Your pricing should reflect that difference explicitly.</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Present packages, not unit prices.</strong> A bundle of three videos typically gets approved faster and pays more per unit than negotiating each video individually. Structure your rates in tiers.</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Establish a floor rate and do not breach it.</strong> You can always negotiate downward from your initial quote in response to scope adjustments. You can almost never negotiate upward once a number is on the table.</li>
        </ol>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">When a brand asks for your rates</h3>
        <blockquote class="border-l-[3px] border-accent pl-5 py-3 my-6 bg-accent-bg rounded-r-lg">
          <p class="text-ink-2 mb-0 text-[15px] leading-[1.7]">
            Here is how I typically price content for a brand like [Brand]:<br><br>
            &bull; 1 UGC video (organic use only): $[X]<br>
            &bull; 1 UGC video + 90-day paid media usage rights: $[X]<br>
            &bull; Package of 3 videos + usage: $[X]<br><br>
            Happy to adjust scope to fit your budget &mdash; let me know what range works and I can put together a tailored proposal.
          </p>
        </blockquote>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">If the brand offers product only</h3>
        <blockquote class="border-l-[3px] border-accent pl-5 py-3 my-6 bg-accent-bg rounded-r-lg">
          <p class="text-ink-2 mb-0 text-[15px] leading-[1.7]">
            Appreciate the offer! I generally work on a paid basis for produced content, but I would be open to a hybrid model &mdash; product plus a flat fee for the deliverables. Would that be something worth exploring?
          </p>
        </blockquote>

        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4"><strong class="text-ink">If the brand goes quiet after receiving your rates:</strong> send Follow-Up 2 from the sequence above. Silence after a rate request usually indicates a budget mismatch, not a lack of interest. Offering a smaller package at a lower total price often restarts the conversation.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 08 Timing -->
    <section class="guide-section" id="timing">
      <div class="max-w-[800px] mx-auto px-6">
        <div class="font-mono text-[11px] font-medium uppercase tracking-[.1em] text-accent mb-2">08 &mdash; Timing</div>
        <h2 class="font-display font-bold text-[clamp(24px,3.5vw,32px)] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink mb-2">When to send for maximum impact</h2>
        <div class="tldr">
          <ul class="list-none p-0 m-0 space-y-1">
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Tuesday through Thursday mornings are the highest-response window</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Align pitches with product launches and budget cycles (Q1, Q3)</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Always send in the recipient's time zone</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">When you send matters almost as much as what you send. Based on what we see across hundreds of brand partnerships at OKAD, certain timing patterns consistently outperform others.</p>

        <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-border bg-card mb-6">
          <table class="w-full border-collapse text-[14px]">
            <thead class="bg-bg">
              <tr>
                <th class="font-display font-semibold text-[13px] px-4 py-3 text-left border-b border-border">Timing factor</th>
                <th class="font-display font-semibold text-[13px] px-4 py-3 text-left border-b border-border">Best window</th>
                <th class="font-display font-semibold text-[13px] px-4 py-3 text-left border-b border-border">Why</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink">Day of week</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">Tue&ndash;Thu mornings</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">Monday inboxes are flooded; Friday gets deferred</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink">Quarterly budget</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">Q1 (Jan) &amp; Q3 (late summer)</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">New annual budgets; Q4 holiday planning starts</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink">Product launches</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">2&ndash;4 weeks before</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">Brands actively looking for content</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink">Time zone</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">9:00&ndash;10:00 AM local</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">Dramatically higher open rates vs 2:00 AM arrival</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-ink">Industry events</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-ink-2">A few days after</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-ink-2">Avoid the noise; catch them when it subsides</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 09 Subject Lines -->
    <section class="guide-section" id="subjects">
      <div class="max-w-[800px] mx-auto px-6">
        <div class="font-mono text-[11px] font-medium uppercase tracking-[.1em] text-accent mb-2">09 &mdash; Subject Lines</div>
        <h2 class="font-display font-bold text-[clamp(24px,3.5vw,32px)] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink mb-2">Subject lines that get opened</h2>
        <div class="tldr">
          <ul class="list-none p-0 m-0 space-y-1">
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Clarity beats cleverness every single time</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">The subject line has one job: earn the open</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Short, specific, and professional</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">Your subject line is a filter. If it reads like spam, it gets treated like spam &mdash; either by the recipient's brain or their literal email filters.</p>

        <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-border bg-card mb-6">
          <table class="w-full border-collapse text-[14px]">
            <thead class="bg-bg">
              <tr>
                <th class="font-display font-semibold text-[13px] px-4 py-3 text-left border-b border-border">Subject lines that perform</th>
                <th class="font-display font-semibold text-[13px] px-4 py-3 text-left border-b border-border">Subject lines to avoid</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink">Content concept for [Brand]</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">EXCITING OPPORTUNITY!!!</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink">Paid partnership idea &mdash; [Your Name]</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">Collab??</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink">Already a customer of [Product] &mdash; quick idea</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">URGENT: partnership inquiry</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink">Applying &mdash; UGC creator for [campaign name]</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">Influencer proposal &mdash; please review</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-ink">Creator content for your Q4 campaigns</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-ink-2">Hi I love your brand</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">The subject line has one job: earn the open. It does not need to sell the partnership, explain your background, or convey enthusiasm. Short, specific, and professional.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 10 Common Mistakes -->
    <section class="guide-section" id="mistakes">
      <div class="max-w-[800px] mx-auto px-6">
        <div class="font-mono text-[11px] font-medium uppercase tracking-[.1em] text-accent mb-2">10 &mdash; Mistakes</div>
        <h2 class="font-display font-bold text-[clamp(24px,3.5vw,32px)] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink mb-2">Errors that kill your response rate</h2>
        <div class="tldr">
          <ul class="list-none p-0 m-0 space-y-1">
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Mass-blasting identical messages is the fastest way to get ignored</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">No portfolio link (or a broken one) kills more pitches than any other factor</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Giving up after one email and going silent after rate objections</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">After reviewing thousands of creator pitches that flow through brand inboxes, these are the errors we see sabotaging otherwise capable creators:</p>

        <ol class="list-none p-0 counter-reset-step space-y-0">
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Sending identical messages to dozens of brands.</strong> Marketing managers can recognize a mass-blast instantly. One specific sentence about the brand is worth more than three paragraphs of generic flattery.</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Hiding the ask.</strong> If the reader has to guess what you want, they will not bother. State whether you are proposing paid content, affiliate work, whitelisting, or something else &mdash; explicitly and early.</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Including no portfolio link, or a broken one.</strong> This single issue kills more promising pitches than any other factor. Test your link before you send. Then test it again on mobile.</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Leading with follower metrics instead of content quality.</strong> UGC arrangements are content purchases, not reach deals. Frame your pitch around what you produce, not how many people watch your personal feed.</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Writing an essay when a paragraph would suffice.</strong> Your initial message should generate curiosity and establish credibility, not explain the entire scope of a potential relationship.</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Giving up after one unanswered email.</strong> Most positive responses come from the first or second follow-up, not the initial message. Build follow-ups into your system from the start.</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Going silent after a rate objection.</strong> A brief, professional counter-proposal &mdash; a smaller package, adjusted usage terms, or a pilot project &mdash; frequently saves deals that seemed dead.</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Negotiating full deals inside DM threads.</strong> Social media messages are for opening doors. Contracts, rates, and deliverable details belong in email where they can be referenced, forwarded, and tracked properly.</li>
        </ol>

        <div class="bg-card border border-border rounded-xl p-6 my-6 flex gap-4 items-start flex-col sm:flex-row">
          <div class="w-11 h-11 rounded-[10px] bg-[rgba(99,102,241,.06)] border border-[rgba(99,102,241,.12)] flex items-center justify-center text-[20px] shrink-0">&#9889;</div>
          <div>
            <h4 class="font-display text-[16px] font-semibold mb-1"><a href={emailPitch} class="text-ink hover:text-accent transition-colors no-underline">OKAD helps creators build outreach systems that work</a></h4>
            <p class="text-[14px] text-muted m-0 leading-[1.5]">Portfolio strategy, contact research, pitch templates, follow-up cadences, and rate negotiation coaching. We help creators land more brand deals without guesswork.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">Pitching is a numbers game &mdash; until your reputation takes over</h3>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">Even a beautifully crafted pitch will not convert every time. Expect single-digit reply rates on cold outreach when you are starting out, gradually improving as your portfolio strengthens and past brand names build social proof. This is precisely why consistency and volume beat a single "perfect" message: the creators landing the most deals are the ones running the most well-targeted outreach, not the ones spending an hour perfecting one email.</p>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">Over time, the equation shifts. Brands start coming to you. Past clients return for repeat projects. Referrals arrive. But that flywheel only starts spinning once you have done the manual work of proving the system, building the relationships, and establishing a track record that speaks for itself.</p>
      </div>
